// THE MIRROR DIRECTION OF trait_ident_cross_archive_bound_refused: the bound is
// two archives away, and the homonym that owns the SPELLING here is the one the
// bound does NOT mean.
//
// Shape (the same three packages as tests/logos/pass/trait_ident_pkg_chain.logos):
//   trait_ident_chain.lhom  declares its own `trait Hash`, `impl Hash for f64`,
//                           and `pub fn hmid_tag`'s bound lives over it
//   trait_ident_chain.hmid  `depends lhom`, no prelude, re-exports `hmid_tag<T: Hash>`
//   THIS package            uses hmid only — lhom is never in scope here
//
// `i64` implements the STDLIB `Hash` and not lhom's, so `hmid_tag(5i64)` must be
// REFUSED. The refusal has to name the trait the BOUND meant — lhom's, written
// in hmid's scope — not the one this file's spelling would reach.
//
// ⚠ WHY THIS FIXTURE EXISTS AND THE pkg_chain ONE DOES NOT PIN IT. That file's
// header records this call as ADMITTED and dying in the MLIR verifier at
//     error: 'func.call' op 'i64__tag' does not reference a valid function
// and deliberately does not assert it: a `fail/` door holding a verifier crash
// would be asserting the defect. Re-measured at 14c36a77f: sema refuses it
// there already (B-mv-03 carried the identity onto the bound), and at
// #438 step 4 it refuses with the ground below, naming both traits. The door
// is now a refusal, so it can be pinned — and what it guards is the direction
// where the local spelling and the bound's identity disagree.
package trait_ident_chain_mirror_bound_refused;

use trait_ident_chain.hmid;

fn main() -> i32 {
    let bad: i64 = hmid_tag(5i64);
    if bad == -1i64 { return 9i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
